=== AGENT (turn 1) — 202.3s, in=17 out=8566, 22 tool calls ===
Here's the architectural plan for your **Customer Email Escalation** flow. I've designed it around the connectors that are already live on your tenant — no new connections needed.

---

## What the flow does

1. **Trigger** — fires the moment a new email lands in your Outlook 365 Inbox (live connector, default connection already enabled)
2. **Classify** — a Script node scores the email's urgency using keyword signals (`urgent`, `outage`, `critical`, `billing`, `refund`, `legal`, `escalate`, `ASAP`) and returns `{ isUrgent, reason, priority }`
3. **Route (Decision node)**
   - **Urgent path →** posts a formatted Slack alert to your support channel (live Slack connector, default connection already enabled) → then sends the customer an immediate acknowledgement reply via Outlook
   - **Standard path →** sends the customer a standard acknowledgement reply → marks the email as read so it doesn't get lost
4. **Error handler** — any failure in classify or Slack is caught and logged on a separate terminal path so the flow never crashes silently

---

## Flow Diagram

```mermaid
graph LR
    trigger(Email Received)
    classifyEmail[Classify Email Urgency]
    checkUrgent{Is Urgent}
    slackAlert[Slack Alert to Team]
    replyUrgent[Reply to Customer Urgent]
    replyStandard[Reply to Customer Standard]
    markRead[Mark Email as Read]
    logError[Log Error]
    doneUrgent(Done Urgent)
    doneStandard(Done Standard)
    doneError(Done Error)
    trigger -->|output| classifyEmail
    classifyEmail -->|success| checkUrgent
    classifyEmail -->|error| logError
    checkUrgent -->|true| slackAlert
    checkUrgent -->|false| replyStandard
    slackAlert -->|output| replyUrgent
    slackAlert -->|error| logError
    replyUrgent -->|output| doneUrgent
    replyStandard -->|output| markRead
    markRead -->|output| doneStandard
    logError -->|success| doneError
```

---

